Title: eager local-main sync: rule + mechanism
Created: 2026-07-11
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-07-11T08:03Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

Author is tired of stale local main. On 2026-07-11 a "sync local main"
request against the primary checkout fast-forwarded another session's
feature branch instead — the checkout was not on main, and the sync idiom
assumed it was. The lesson was saved as memory
(feedback_sync_main_check_primary_branch_first); the author asked to
promote it to a rule and to make the sync eager.

## Actions

1. `scripts/sync-local-main.sh` — safe sync callable from any worktree:
   moves only the default branch, ff-only, by ref when unchecked-out,
   ff-merge in the one worktree holding it, reports (never touches)
   dirty-overlap or diverged state; always exit 0 (hook-safe).
2. `scripts/on-start.sh` — call it backgrounded (timeout 60) at session
   start, in the silent section.
3. `skills/merge/SKILL.md` — after the merge is confirmed landed, run the
   sync script; forbid the hand-rolled `merge --ff-only` on the primary.
4. `rules/git.md` — new bullet "Local main syncs eagerly, by ref — never
   by assumption" naming the failure mode and the script.
5. Memory entry updated to point at the rule.

## Test

- `tests/test_sync_local_main.sh` (auto-discovered by test_bash_suites):
  ff on clean checked-out main; ref update with feature checked out and
  feature untouched; dirty overlap preserved and reported; diverged main
  untouched; sync from a linked worktree; silent when in sync; no-origin
  and non-repo skips.

## Verification

- [ ] Test suite green under `make check`
- [ ] `make check-fast` green; pipefail/agnostic guards pass

## Invariants

- The script never moves a non-default branch, never discards or stashes
  working-tree state, never force-updates a diverged main.
- Session start must not block on network (backgrounded + timeout).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Rule bullet in rules/git.md; script + call sites merged
- [ ] Local main is refreshed at session start and post-merge without
      manual intervention
